{"name":"Silver Surfer","hex":"#7e7d88","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver surfer","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elderly person who regularly uses the Internet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_surfer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Surfer","blocks":[{"text":"The Silver Surfer is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics. The character also appears in a number of movies, television, and video game adaptations. The character was created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby and first appeared in the comic book Fantastic Four #48, published in 1966. The Silver Surfer is a humanoid alien with metallic skin who can travel through space with the aid of his surfboard-like craft. Originally a young astronomer named Norrin Radd on the planet Zenn-La, he saves his homeworld from the planet devourer, Galactus, by serving as his herald. Imbued in return with some portion of Galactus's Power Cosmic, he acquires vast power, a new body and a surfboard-like craft on which he can travel faster than light."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/34/Silver_Surfer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Surfer"},{"title":"Silver Surfer (comic book)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Surfer or The Silver Surfer is the name of several series of comic books published by Marvel Comics featuring the Silver Surfer."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9e/Silver_Surfer_01_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Surfer_(comic_book)"},{"title":"Surfing","blocks":[{"text":"Surfing is a surface water sport in which an individual, a surfer, uses a board to ride on the forward section, or face, of a moving wave of water, which usually carries the surfer towards the shore. Waves suitable for surfing are primarily found on ocean shores, but can also be found as standing waves in the open ocean, in lakes, in rivers in the form of a tidal bore, or wave pools."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Mavericks_Surf_Contest_2010b.jpg/330px-Mavericks_Surf_Contest_2010b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surfing"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Surf-riding_1858.jpg/500px-Surf-riding_1858.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Surf-riding_1858.jpg/1280px-Surf-riding_1858.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Surf-riding_1858.jpg/500px-Surf-riding_1858.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1858 illustration of \"surf-riding\" in Hawaii.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASurf-riding_1858.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Huanchaco.jpg/500px-Huanchaco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Huanchaco.jpg/1280px-Huanchaco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Huanchaco.jpg/500px-Huanchaco.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Caballitos de totora, reed watercraft used by fishermen for the past 3000 years at Huanchaco, Peru, known for its surf breaks","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuanchaco.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Essie_Harris_Surfs_the_cold_waters_of_Morro_Bay%2C_CA_14_Oct_2009.jpg/500px-Essie_Harris_Surfs_the_cold_waters_of_Morro_Bay%2C_CA_14_Oct_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Essie_Harris_Surfs_the_cold_waters_of_Morro_Bay%2C_CA_14_Oct_2009.jpg/1280px-Essie_Harris_Surfs_the_cold_waters_of_Morro_Bay%2C_CA_14_Oct_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Essie_Harris_Surfs_the_cold_waters_of_Morro_Bay%2C_CA_14_Oct_2009.jpg/500px-Essie_Harris_Surfs_the_cold_waters_of_Morro_Bay%2C_CA_14_Oct_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A woman holding her surfboard about to surf in Morro Bay, California","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEssie_Harris_Surfs_the_cold_waters_of_Morro_Bay%2C_CA_14_Oct_2009.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Pipeline_Barrel_%2851633882%29.jpeg/500px-Pipeline_Barrel_%2851633882%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Pipeline_Barrel_%2851633882%29.jpeg/500px-Pipeline_Barrel_%2851633882%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign